 Recently I have been looking at William Lam&rsquo;s excellent post on automating the deployment of vROps.
After having a play around with it, to suit my own needs, I made some modifications to the Powershell script so it would support distributed switches.
# William Lam
# Edited by Simon Eady to support vDS
# www.virtuallyghetto.com
# Deployment of vRealize Operations Manager 6.0 (vROps)
### NOTE: SSH can not be enabled because hidden properties do not seem to be implemented in Get-OvfConfiguration cmdlet ###
# Load OVF/OVA configuration into a variable
$ovffile = &ldquo;E:\automation\vRealize-Operations-Manager-Appliance-6.0.1.2523163_OVF10.ova&rdquo;
$ovfconfig = Get-OvfConfiguration $ovffile
# vSphere Cluster + VM Network configurations
$Cluster = &ldquo;Name of Cluster&rdquo;
$VMName = &ldquo;Name of vROps VM&rdquo;
$VDS = &ldquo;Enter vDS here&rdquo;
$VDSPG = &ldquo;Enter vDS portgroup here&rdquo;
$VCServer = &ldquo;Enter vCenter here&rdquo;
$VMHost = Get-Cluster $Cluster | Get-VMHost | Sort MemoryGB | Select -first 1
$Datastore = $VMHost | Get-datastore | Sort FreeSpaceGB -Descending | Select -first 1
$Network = Get-VDPortgroup -VDSwitch $VDS -Name $VDSPG -Server $VCServer
# Fill out the OVF/OVA configuration parameters
# xsmall,small,medium,large,smallrc,largerc
$ovfconfig.DeploymentOption.value = &ldquo;xsmall&rdquo;
# IP Address
$ovfConfig.vami.vRealize_Operations_Manager_Appliance.ip0.value = &ldquo;desired ip of vROps&rdquo;
# Gateway
$ovfConfig.vami.vRealize_Operations_Manager_Appliance.gateway.value = &ldquo;gateway for environment&rdquo;
# Netmask
$ovfConfig.vami.vRealize_Operations_Manager_Appliance.netmask0.value = &ldquo;Subnet mask for your environment&rdquo;
# DNS
$ovfConfig.vami.vRealize_Operations_Manager_Appliance.DNS.value = &ldquo;DNS server IP&rdquo;
# vSphere Portgroup Network Mapping
$ovfconfig.NetworkMapping.Network_1.value = $Network
# IP Protocol
$ovfconfig.IpAssignment.IpProtocol.value = &ldquo;IPv4&rdquo;
# Timezone
$ovfconfig.common.vamitimezone.value = &ldquo;Etc/UTC&rdquo;
# Deploy the OVF/OVA with the config parameters
Import-VApp -Source $ovffile -OvfConfiguration $ovfconfig -Name $VMName -VMHost $vmhost -Datastore $datastore -DiskStorageFormat thin
If it&rsquo;s of use please go thank William he did the hard work on this one.

Today I was configuring a new FTP server based on IIS7 (well, 7.5 technically as it’s a Server 2008 R2 host), and I wanted an easy way to add and remove allowed IP addresses based on either an XML config file or a CSV import. Customers’ IP addresses are added or removed regularly, but I didn’t want to have to update their details twice, once on the server and once in the documents.
I figured that the PowerShell extensions for managing IIS would do nicely – they are great for managing IIS sites after all and the idea with FTP in IIS7 is that it doesn’t care which protocol you are using, HTTP, HTTPS, FTP or FTP over SSL – all should be configured the same way.
The security setup had to follow the policy that all IP addresses are denied unless explicitly allowed.
I imported the module and listed the cmdlets:
Import-Module –Name WebAdministration
Get-Command –PSSnapin WebAdministration
Creating a new FTP site # This is very, very simple with PowerShell
New-WebFtpSite -Name &lsquo;FTP Site&rsquo; -IPAddress &lsquo;192.168.10.22&rsquo; -HostHeader &lsquo;ftp.mcgeown.co.uk&rsquo; -PhysicalPath &rsquo;d:\FTP\
A good start then!
By default, the site will allow any IP address to access the server – it’s time to start locking it down.
Allow Authorised Users # Add-WebConfiguration -Filter /system.ftpserver/security/authorization -PSPath &lsquo;IIS:' -Value @{accessType=&lsquo;Allow&rsquo;;users=&rsquo;*&rsquo;;permissions=3} -Location &lsquo;FTP Site&rsquo; Restricting IP Access # Unfortunately the IP Address Restrictions part of the configuration isn’t exposed directly by a cmdlet so I thought I’d use one or two of the lower level IIS configuration cmdlets – Add-WebConfiguration, and Set-WebConfigurationProperty. And after a lot of fiddling and a lot of help by a colleague, I stumbled upon the correct syntax:
Set-WebConfigurationProperty -Filter /system.ftpserver/security/ipsecurity -Name allowUnlisted -Value $false -Location &lsquo;FTP Site&rsquo; -PSPath &lsquo;IIS:'
Add-WebConfiguration -Filter /system.ftpserver/security/ipsecurity -PSPath &lsquo;IIS:' -Value @{ipAddress=’192.168.1.1’;subnetMask=’255.255.255.255’;allowed=$true} –Location ‘FTP Site’
The following code is appended to the end of the applicationHost.config file – now hopefully you can see that the crazy colours relate to the section in the same colour on below:
&laquo;span style=&ldquo;color: #ff00ff&rdquo;&gt;location path=&ldquo;FTP Site&rdquo; &gt;
&laquo;span style=&ldquo;color: #004000&rdquo;&gt;system.ftpServer&gt;
&laquo;span style=&ldquo;color: #008000&rdquo;&gt;security&gt;
&laquo;span style=&ldquo;color: #00ff00&rdquo;&gt;ipSecurity allowUnlisted=&ldquo;false&rdquo;&gt;
&laquo;span style=&ldquo;color: #ff8000&rdquo;&gt;add ipAddress=&ldquo;192.168.1.1&rdquo; allowed=&ldquo;true&rdquo; /&gt;
&lt;/ipSecurity&gt;
&laquo;span style=&ldquo;color: #80ff00&rdquo;&gt;authorization&gt;
&laquo;span style=&ldquo;color: #8000ff&rdquo;&gt;add accessType=&ldquo;Allow&rdquo; users=&quot;*&quot; permissions=&ldquo;Read, Write&rdquo; /&gt;
&lt;/authorization&gt;
&lt;/security&gt;
&lt;/system.ftpServer&gt;
&lt;/location&gt;
Scripting the whole thing # I’ve created two files, Add-AllowedIPs.ps1 and AllowedIPs.config. The config file is just an XML structure with the allowed IPs and Site name:
<?xml version="1.0"?> 192.168.8.1 255.255.255.255 10.10.10.0 255.255.254.0 The ps1 file is a simple script to read the configuration and apply the settings:
$ConfigFile= &ldquo;AllowedIPs.config&rdquo;
if(Test-Path $ConfigFile){
\[xml\]$Config = Get-Content $ConfigFile}else{throw (&ldquo;Unable to find configuration file: &quot; + $ConfigFile)}
$Site = $Config.Configuration.Site.Name
Write-Host &ldquo;Clearing $Site IP Security Lists&rdquo; -BackgroundColor Yellow -ForegroundColor DarkBlue
Clear-WebConfiguration -Filter /system.ftpserver/security/ipsecurity -PSPath &lsquo;IIS:' -Location &ldquo;$Site&rdquo;
Write-Host &ldquo;Adding Allowed IPs to $Site&rdquo; -BackgroundColor Yellow -ForegroundColor DarkBlue
$Config.Configuration.Site.Address | foreach {
$IP = $.IP.Trim()
$SubnetMask = $.SubnetMask.Trim()
Add-WebConfiguration -Filter /system.ftpserver/security/ipsecurity -PSPath &lsquo;IIS:' -Location &ldquo;$Site&rdquo; -Value @{ipAddress=&quot;$IP&rdquo;;subnetMask=&quot;$SubnetMask&quot;;allowed=$true}
}
Not quite sure why doing something so simple should have occupied so much of my time, but I hope it saves you some!
Sam

Our development SQL server is a monster…there are many many databases, and hundreds, if not thousands of backup files. With each patch tested on the software we sell, there is a new backup. With each client deployment, a new database. With each new major version, a new database. Backups of the new databases inevitably occur, and so we have more files, in more folders - most of which need to be kept in case of roll-backs, bugs or deployment issues.
This all adds up to a bit of an administrative nightmare, especially since the backups eat away at my storage at a phenomonal rate. Zipping the .bak files is great, but since each DB has it&rsquo;s own backup folder, it can become a bit of a nightmare to go through, zip and delete the .baks. For my first real foray into using PowerShell, I decided I&rsquo;d write a script to take the legwork out of it for me.
`
# Powershell Script to recurse input path looking for .bak files, Zip them
and delete the .bak.
# function out-zip {
Param([string]$path)
if (-not $path.EndsWith(&rsquo;.zip&rsquo;)) {$path += &lsquo;.zip&rsquo;}
if (-not (test-path $path)) {
set-content $path (&ldquo;PK&rdquo; + [char]5 + [char]6 + (&quot;$([char]0)&quot; * 18))
}
$ZipFile = (new-object -com shell.application).NameSpace($path)
$input | foreach {$zipfile.CopyHere($_.fullname)} | out-null
}
$FileCount =0
$FilesZipped =0
$FilesDeleted =0
$InputPath = $args[0]
if($InputPath.Length -lt 2)
{
Write-Host &ldquo;Please supply a path name as your first argument&rdquo; -foregroundcolor Red
return
}
if(-not (Test-Path $InputPath))
{
Write-Host &ldquo;Path does not appear to be valid&rdquo; -foregroundcolor Red
return
}
$BakFiles = Get-ChildItem $InputPath -Include *.bak -recurse
Foreach ($Bak in $BakFiles)
{
write-host &ldquo;File: $Bak&rdquo; -foregroundcolor Yellow
$ZipFile = $Bak.FullName -replace &ldquo;.bak&rdquo;, &ldquo;.zip&rdquo;
if (Test-Path $ZipFile)
{
Write-Host &ldquo;$ZipFile exists already, aborted.&rdquo; -foregroundcolor Red
}
else
{
Get-Item $Bak | out-zip $ZipFile
if(Test-Path $ZipFile)
{
$Response = read-host -prompt &ldquo;Please wait for zip to complete then type c to continue&hellip;&quot;
if($Response = &ldquo;c&rdquo;)
{
$FilesZipped++
Remove-Item $Bak.FullName
if(Test-Path $Bak.FullName)
{
Write-Host &ldquo;File not deleted, manually remove $Bak.Fullname&rdquo; -foregroundcolor Red
}
else
{
Write-Host &ldquo;OK&rdquo; -foregroundcolor Green
$FilesDeleted++
}
}
else
{
Write-Host &ldquo;File delete aborted by user&rdquo; -foregroundcolor Red
}
}
}
$FileCount++
}
Write-Host Files found: $FileCount
Write-Host Files Zipped: $FilesZipped
Write-Host Files Deleted: $FilesDeleted
`
Obviously, this is not something I&rsquo;d recommend you running lightly without serious testing on your own systems - that said, I hope it helps! I make no warantee or any kind of promise that you won&rsquo;t lose data by running this! It&rsquo;s just an exercise in PowerShell for me.
